Hard rules (orchestrator-house, derived from canon — surfaced so you don't miss them):
- **`class="scene clip"` on the scene div** — `clip` is required by HF lint on any element with `data-start`/`data-duration` (the runtime keys hard-cut visibility off `.clip`). Standalone-composition canon (`transitions/catalog.md` L13) omits both timing attrs *and* the `clip` class on scene divs because root timing covers them; our orchestrator-house Pattern A keeps timing on each scene for the v4 visibility shim, so `clip` MUST come along.
- **CSS scoping** — every rule prefixed with `#scene-{{ scene_id }}`. No bare class selectors. Inlining is collision-prone otherwise.
- **`tl.fromTo()` only** for entrances — never `tl.from()`, never bare `gsap.from()`. Non-linear seek breaks otherwise.
- **No `data-composition-id` on the scene div** — that attribute belongs to the root composition only (Pattern A; see catalog.md L13).
- **No `<template>` wrapper.** Pattern B (sub-compositions) is broken upstream; this fragment is inlined as-is.
- **No `<script src=".../gsap...">`** — root scaffold supplies GSAP once.
- **IIFE-wrap the entire script body** (exactly as in the output shape above). All scenes are inlined into the same root `index.html`, and HTML `<script>` blocks share one script-lexical environment — an un-IIFE'd `const tl` collides with the scaffold's top-level `const tl` and every other scene, halting `hyperframes validate` with `Identifier 'tl' has already been declared`. The `__sceneTimelines["…"] = tl` registration MUST live inside the IIFE.
- **Density floor**: ≥ 8 elements visible at the hero frame, ≥ 2 BG decoratives with ambient motion (`tl.to()` on the seekable `tl`, never bare `gsap.to()`).
- **≥ 3 distinct eases** (Animation Guardrails); first entrance offset 0.1–0.3s.
- **Final-fade (HR 4)**: permitted iff `is_final == True`. Otherwise NO `tl.to(..., {opacity: 0})` on the scene container — scene-to-scene transitions are root-scope and authored elsewhere.
- **NO infinite repeats** — the GSAP infinite-repeat sentinel is forbidden (canon: `~/.agents/skills/hyperframes/SKILL.md` §"Animation Guardrails" L227 + Rules L240). Infinite-repeat timelines break the deterministic capture engine. For any looping ambient motion, compute a FINITE count from the scene's `data-duration` and the tween's own duration:
  ```js
  const sceneDuration = {{ data_duration_s }};
  const cycleDuration = 1.2; // your tween duration in seconds
  tl.to(".aura", { scale: 1.08, yoyo: true,
                   repeat: Math.floor(sceneDuration / cycleDuration),
                   duration: cycleDuration }, 0);
  ```
  Never write the literal token `repeat:` followed by the infinite sentinel — even inside JS comments — HF lint regex false-positives on the bare substring (memory `feedback_lint_regex_repeat_minus_one_in_comments`). Use a finite count (`Math.floor(...)`, or a large positive integer like `repeat: 999` for unbounded-feeling ambient motion).
- **opacity initial state**: `0` for non-first scenes (the future transitions node fades them in). First scene starts visible.
- **Palette discipline (strict ⊆).** Use ONLY hexes from `state.compose.design.palette[*].hex` (already pre-baked with gradient-stop / highlight derivatives by `p4_design_system`). No improvised gradient stops, no shade variants, no near-palette derivatives. The HF canon `gate:design_adherence` (`~/.agents/skills/hyperframes/SKILL.md` §"Design Adherence" item 1) rejects any hex not literally in the palette set. If a needed shade isn't in the palette, choose the nearest palette entry — do NOT invent a 5-6 unit-off-channel variant. If you genuinely need an in-between value, prefer alpha-blending via `rgba(...)` (a literal palette hex parsed to channels + an alpha) or `tl.set/.to(..., {opacity: ...})`; both are gate-clean because the gate hex-extracts only `#RRGGBB` / `#RRGGBBAA` tokens.
  ```css
  /* ❌ ALL three stops missing from palette — gate:design_adherence will fail */
  background: linear-gradient(180deg, #2a221c 0%, #1f1a16 55%, #14100e 100%);
  /* ✅ every stop is a literal palette[*].hex — pre-baked by p4_design_system */
  background: linear-gradient(180deg, var(--background) 0%, var(--background-stop-1) 55%, var(--background-stop-2) 100%);
  ```
- **Typography fallback discipline.** In any `font-family` stack, use ONLY:
  - named families from `state.compose.design.typography[*].family` (Playfair Display, Inter — whatever DESIGN.md declares),
  - generic CSS keywords (`serif`, `sans-serif`, `monospace`),
  - the system-UI whitelist (`system-ui`, `-apple-system`).

  Do NOT add named fallbacks like Georgia, Helvetica, Times New Roman. `gate:design_adherence` (`~/.agents/skills/hyperframes/SKILL.md` §"Design Adherence" item 2) flags any token not in `typography[*].family` ∪ generics ∪ whitelist. Web fonts loaded by the root scaffold render reliably across the capture engine; named fallbacks would only fire if web-loading fails (it doesn't), and would render differently on different operator machines (Win/Mac may have Georgia, Linux Chromium resolves to Liberation/DejaVu Serif — divergent video output).
  ```css
  /* ❌ "Georgia" not in typography list — gate:design_adherence will fail */
  font-family: "Playfair Display", "Georgia", serif;
  /* ✅ design family + generic keyword */
  font-family: "Playfair Display", serif;
  ```

Explicit anti-patterns (DO NOT DO):

These are patterns the LLM keeps re-introducing despite canon. Each iteration of the lint gate that catches them costs a redispatch — preventing them up front converges Phase 4 in fewer beats.

1. **GSAP repeat math — use `Math.floor`, not `Math.ceil`.** Scenes have a fixed `data-duration`; if the total animation length (`(repeat + 1) * cycleDuration`) exceeds it, the capture engine truncates mid-cycle and a visible jump appears at scene boundary.
   ```js
   // ❌ overshoots scene duration — visible jump at the cut
   repeat: Math.ceil(sceneDuration / cycleDuration) - 1
   // ❌ also overshoots (Math.ceil rounds up)
   repeat: Math.ceil(sceneDuration / cycleDuration)
   // ✅ total animation never exceeds scene
   repeat: Math.floor(sceneDuration / cycleDuration)
   ```
   Canon: `~/.agents/skills/hyperframes/references/motion-principles.md` — bullet "Hard-kill every scene boundary, not just captions." (the same principle: nothing animation-related may bleed past `data-duration`). Read it before writing repeat math.

2. **Caption / element exit MUST be followed by a `tl.set(visibility: "hidden")` kill at fade-end.** A bare `tl.to(opacity: 0)` leaves the element in DOM at `opacity: 0` — later tweens (or sibling `immediateRender`) can resurrect it, and the element still participates in layout calc.
   ```js
   // ❌ fade alone — element can resurrect, still occupies layout
   tl.to(captionEl, { opacity: 0, duration: 0.3 }, fadeStart);
   // ✅ fade + deterministic kill at fade-end
   tl.to(captionEl, { opacity: 0, duration: 0.3 }, fadeStart);
   tl.set(captionEl, { visibility: "hidden" }, fadeStart + 0.3);
   ```
   Canon: `~/.agents/skills/hyperframes/references/captions.md` §"Caption Exit Guarantee" and `~/.agents/skills/hyperframes/references/motion-principles.md` — bullet "Hard-kill every scene boundary, not just captions." Read both BEFORE authoring any element with an exit animation. The principle generalises beyond captions: every element with a fade-out needs a paired `tl.set(... visibility: "hidden")` kill at the fade-end timestamp.

